{"qid":"Q28322","names":["Balhae"],"title":"Balhae","endonym":{"native":"진국","lang":"ko"},"capsule":"Balhae (Chinese: Bohai) was a multiethnic state in Manchuria, the Russian Maritime Province, and northern Korea, founded in 698 by the Mohe chieftain Dae Joyeong using remnants of the defeated Goguryeo kingdom as its administrative backbone. Recognized by Tang China as the 'State of Bohai' in 713, it organized a five-capital administrative system modeled on Tang institutions and maintained regular diplomatic and trade missions to Japan. At its mid-9th-century apex Balhae encompassed fifteen circuits and was described by Tang sources as 'the flourishing land of the east.' The Khitan-led Liao dynasty destroyed Balhae in 926, relocating much of its elite population; survivors who fled south were absorbed into the Goryeo kingdom.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The five-capital core area is reasonably well-documented from Tang and Goryeo chronicles and from ongoing Chinese and Russian archaeological work; peripheral circuit boundaries are reconstructed from administrative texts and are approximate, especially in the north and east toward the Amur basin.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Manchuria — History (Bohai kingdom)","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Manchuria/History"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Balhae","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balhae"}],"britannica":"place/Parhae","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Balhae","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Go of Balhae (Dae Joyeong)","nativeName":"大祚榮","roman":"Dae Joyeong","start":698,"end":719,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid"